A||||11||p4n1c||Question about an article||||1||
Z||000000||p4n1c||12-20-2000||11:55 AM||||Just need some peoples opinions on this, last night I took a quick look through the car magazines in chapters.  Lo and behold there was a Pontiac magazine, I started to flip through it and found only one article(can't really even call it an article, it was more a Q&A letter) on a Fiero GT.<P>The guy was complaining about how he shut his door on his GT(didn't mention the year) and the sail window cracked(That would be the quarter window, correct?), and he heard that this was a common occurance with them.  The answer from the magazine was for the guy to replace the hinge pins, because the door is off kilter(or something along that lines), but he would be hard pressed to find parts(???), so to use some from a 84-92 S-10 I believe(sorry I didn't buy the magazine, not enough useful content to make me want it).<P>Would stating that parts for the Fiero are hard to find be in error?  I find they are a lot easier to find then parts for some cars.<P>One thing popped up in my magazine tour, in the latest Kit Car magazine, there is a few page article on a Ferrari kit ontop of a Fiero chassis.<BR><P>------------------<BR>--<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||12-20-2000||12:07 PM||||I think without a doubt if the S10 pins fit, they would be easier to source then Fiero. There are a gajillion S10's in service.||||reg||1
Z||000002||p4n1c||12-20-2000||12:13 PM||||True enough.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  But the way this answer was worded in the magazine made it seem like  you would have to cut your own arm off and sell your soul to the little guy on the corner claiming he's Satan to get them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>--<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'||||reg||7
Z||000003||Tigger||12-20-2000||12:50 PM||||I don't know if it's Deja Vu, but I think I read that too.<P>Likely the hinge bushings were bad.  I would say there is a real good chance the S-10 use the same size bushings.<P>Hehe, it doesn't bother me if they want to make everyone think Fiero parts are hard to find.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||p4n1c||12-20-2000||12:55 PM||||Very true.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  That leaves more parts for us.<BR><P>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'||||reg||10
Z||000005||RiceCooker||12-20-2000||02:41 PM||||Common Occurrence?<P>Has anyone else had this happen? Just wondering||||reg||5
Z||000006||p4n1c||12-20-2000||02:47 PM||||Yeah, that was another thing I was wondering, I haven't read anything on this forum along the lines of that problem.  But then again he might only know one other guy with a Fiero and that happened.  Or maybe he made it up, because he was drunk put his head through the window and couldn't remember in the morning.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 12-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||12-20-2000||03:00 PM||||Common to all cars with over 100k miles, I've seen it on a few Fieros.  It's caused by people never oiling their door hinges.   Dirt and stuff slowly wears away the bushings eventually whopping them out and making the door sag.||||reg||1
Z||000008||p4n1c||12-20-2000||03:05 PM||||My parents Cutlass had a sagging door problem, but it was because there was a recall on the hinges, that they never decided to tell them about until this year.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  They got brand spankin' new ones and the door sags no more.<P>But now I know to oil the hinges regularly to prevent this from happening when I get my car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'||||reg||1
Z||000009||firstfiero||12-20-2000||03:08 PM||||   Good info...My bushings are bad in mine. The driver side door does sag!  thanks||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||12-20-2000||06:23 PM||||Ya, kitkar mag is reason I build my Ferrari/Fiero. You can buy hinge pin kits for almost any car, some only need slightly modified. Worse than Fiero is Ford Explorer, I replace hinges 3 or 4 times a month on them, Im now an expert, lol. One way to fix em to last longer is get oversize pins, drill them out and put in brass bushing kit.<BR>           <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556367">     <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||12-20-2000||07:46 PM||||There is an article on door service, including the hinge pins, in my cave.<P><A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A><P>The hing pins are about $9 per door.<P>Why it cracked the sail I don't know. The notch back sail is fairly loose. The fastback one I don't know much about. Might have been ready to crack and just needed an excuse. Plastic is often like that.||||reg||1
